NotesString/You can open this notes window for any project by selecting View->Notes...

This example shows the ISO7816-3 / Smart Card / SIM interpreter decoding the "Answer to
Reset" (ATR) response from a typical SIM card.

A cursor has been place next to each of several points of interest in this captured data. 
From within the Waveforms or State List tabs, you can quickly scroll to cursors A through F 
by pressing the <A> through <F> keys on your keyboard. 

You can quickly zoom in or out by pressing the <+> or <-> keys on the numerical 
keypad of your keyboard. 

By zooming in near each of the cursors, the following sequence can be observed:

At cursor A the TS initial character value of 3Bh is transferred. Its value indicates "Direct" 
convention is being used. Cursor B has been used to measure the start bit interval of 
103.9us indicating a baud rate of 9624. The interpreter has been set accordingly.

At cursor C the T0 format character value of 9Fh is transferred. Its upper nibble value of 9 
indicates that at least the TA1 and TD1 interface characters will be transferred. Its lower nibble 
value of Fh indicates that 15 "historical" characters T1 through T15 will also be transferred.

At cursor D a string of interface characters is transferred as follows:

TA1 transferred with a value of 95h. Upper nibble value of 9 indicates clock rate conversion 
factor "F" is 512. Lower nibble value of 5 indicates the bit rate adjustment factor "D" is 15. 

TD1 transferred with a value of 80h. Upper nibble value of 8 indicates that additional 
character TD2 will be transferred. 

TD2  transferred with a value of 1Fh. Upper nibble value of 1 indicates that additional 
character TA2 will be transferred. 

TA2 character is transferred with value of C7h.

At cursor E the string of "historical" characters T1 through T15 is transferred.

Finally at cursor F, because interface character TA2 was included, the transmission is 
terminated with the TCK character. Its value of F4h is the result of consecutively XOR'ing 
all characters from T0 to T15 (Characters from cursor B up to but not including the TCK 
character). When the TCK value of F4h is XOR'd with that result, the final value
becomes 0, indicating successful transmission.

Please note that all of the example files are "read only" so that they remain intact
for future reference. You can change settings in these files for learning purposes, 
but the changes cannot be saved. You can however use the "Save As" option to 
save an editable copy of any example file.

/
